Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  around 66 million years ago  an asteroid struck our planet and  triggered a mass extinction that wiped  out all non-avian dinosaurs  but what if it hadn't what would our  world look like  if the dinosaur dinosaurs still ruled  over it this theoretical timeline is the  subject of the speculative zoology book  the new dinosaurs written by dougal  dixon  who popularized speculative zoology with  the trilogy which also includes  after man and man after man that  chronicles evolution in the far  future dixon's work influenced other  series you might know  like the future is wild and cm cozman's  ambitious  all tomorrows but the new dinosaurs is  unique in how it imagines not the future  but an alternative form of the present  so  for this entry into the archive we're  going to catalogue this other world  dixon conceptualized  bearing in mind some of the science is a  bit outdated  and it's of course impossible to know  exactly what such a timeline would look  like  think of this book and by extension this  video  as just an interesting guess so let's  jump in  and imagine the great asteroid was just  another flash of light in the sky  and over the next several million years  the dawn rose on a new order of  dinosaurs  beginning in the continent we know as  africa humans have of course  never evolved instead the first new  dinosaurs we meet  are the strange forest-dwelling  treehoppers  and the long-faced wasp eaters the wasp  beater is similar to our timeline's  anteater  as both species spend time in the trees  and feature  lengthy snouts for reaching into insect  nests  dixon classifies these species as  arbosaurus  and his concept of a tree-dwelling  dinosaur actually came true to an extent  after this book's publication  paleontologists discovered the existence  of  microraptors small pre-dwelling  dinosaurs not far off from what dixon  imagined  moving from the forest to the savannah  we've got the hilariously  named flarp bipedal scavengers descended  from the aerial pterosaurs  like the emu or ostrich we know their  wings are now vestigial  so they're stuck on the ground even more  stuck on the ground  is the lank massive unusual grazers also  descended from pterosaurs  this species is a pretty clear analog to  the giraffe and some paleontologists  have noted it's not the most realistic  endpoint for a species like a pterosaur  but the link are just so funny looking i  can't help but approve of dixon's  creation  and in the desert the last african  species i want to touch on awaits  beneath the sand is the wyrm a super  alarming looking organism dixon imagines  this tube of a creature moves like a  snake  and can burrow under the sands to catch  prey frankly i  wonder if our snakes would be offended  by the comparison  heading north to the continent we know  as europe is a species even further away  from traditional dinosaurs  this is the gestalt a species similar in  size to ground squirrels  but dixon imagines they could build  complex nests  and even form intricate communities  these intelligent dinosaurs are  descended from pachyocephalosaurus  thick gold lizards not really known for  their brains so  it's an incredible transformation  heading to the south we have the  diminutive swim  not a dinosaur at all but a mammal the  species is close to a modern mouse and  is about as big as mammals get in this  alternate reality  this is because mammals were only able  to diversify  into the species we know today because  the dinosaurs declined  which never happened in this alternative  timeline to the north we have the hefty  tromble  similar in statue to ostriches the  tromble is a true bird  a classification which is pretty sparse  in this alternate reality  like the mammals true birds had too much  competition to flourish like they do  today  across the ocean to the region we call  north america  we've got the truly insane looking  sprint asaurus  the surreal dinosaurs are evolved from  the duck-filled dinosaurs of the  cretaceous period  with elaborate crests used for group  identification the sprintosauruses are  similar to the prong-horned antelope and  they can run at high speeds to avoid  predators  and have become fully quadrupedal  meaning they walk on four legs  the sprintosauruses are hunted in turn  by the rather deranged looking northclaw  a theropod with a single massive claw  for hunting prey  but there's one dinosaur species on the  grassland the north claw can't take down  that being the massive herbivorous  monocorn  these huge grazing animals share the  large defensive horns of rhinos but move  across the north american prairies and  great herds like buffalo  descended from the triceratops they're  maintaining their ancestors niche as  large  defensive herbivores heading to the  rainforests of what we call south  america  we've got the scale covered pangaloon a  species with an armored defense similar  to our timeline's pangolins  who for the record are just the best  nearby the massive lumberers continue  the treetop grazing habits of their  sauropod ancestors  although they now feature a trunk much  like our reality's tapers  which both species use to aid in  consuming vegetation  tragically the lumbers are preyed upon  by the fearsome  cutlass tooth a dinosaur whose unique  dental arrangement  resembles our timeline smilodon given  their frightening nature you'd expect  the cutlass tooth to be a t-rex's  descendant  but nope the tyrannosaurus rex evolved  into the rather stumpy looking gourmond  a species which lacks any front limbs at  all i mean hey  in our timeline the tyrannosaurus's  closest living relative  is the chicken so it could be worse  crossing oceans once again to what we  now know as asia we've got the mountain  dwelling tatty  the species covered in insulating  pseudo-feathers with a rather  unfortunate face  the teddy is filling a similar niche to  our reality's macaw a species  of monkeys which thrive in similar  regions the taddy's coloring and tree  swinging abilities also make it similar  to the gibbon  although unlike either monkey species  the taddy is imagined to be not  particularly smart  another species that will haunt my  nightmares the tree worms are an  elongated organism related to the desert  worm discussed in the africa section  and like the desert variety the tree  worm is a snake-like predator  and like many modern snakes possesses  the ability to climb  trees and move along branches in search  of prey pretty  unsettling stuff in a distant valley a  less unsettling but truly unique species  lurks  this is the numbskull a bipedal animal  with a swollen looking head which  almost seems humanoid in its appearance  the idea of dinosaurs eventually  evolving into a species with human-like  intelligence seems fascinating  and has been the subject of numerous  other sci-fi books  as interesting as this seems in theory  it's hard to say how likely it is a  creature with human-like intellect would  ever emerge from dinosaurs  and dixon doesn't seem too convinced  this is why the numbskull living up to  its name  isn't actually too bright further south  we come to australia  a region with plenty of unique animals  in our timeline  and dixon's vision of the place is even  crazier  this is the cribum an algae chomping  species similar in build and coloring to  flamingos  unlike our realities flamingos however  the cribbin can't actually take flight  one species that can at least get some  air through hopping is the guana  the last surviving relation to  aguanodons which hop across the grassy  plains  as you can imagine these dinosaurs have  found a similar role to that of  kangaroos and are even at a similar size  much smaller is the crack beak another  species with an appearance i find deeply  unsettling for reasons i can't quite  explain  but nature is about function not form  and the crackbeat  are quite skilled at cracking open nuts  although the species also eats insects  much like a modern australian kookaburra  and the final australian dinosaur of  note is the cloon  a strangely endearing bipedal herbivore  with a thick woolly coat  although not feeling the same niche the  animal almost reminds me of the capybara  which has a similar face and peaceful  nature  so that might be all seven continents  covered but we're not done with our list  quite yet  we still need to explore what sorts of  species are lurking in this world's  oceans  beginning near the poles we have the  aquatic plunger  a streamlined black and white organism  that's pretty clearly an analog to the  penguin  like most penguins they're described as  adept swimmers with wings modified for  hydrodynamic movement  a pretty creative idea for a species if  a familiar one  four or less familiar however is the  kraken  an aptly named species with a shell that  almost resembles a tiny floating island  the kraken has 12 tentacles with deadly  stingers and will eat anything that  becomes entangled in its traps  as you can imagine the kraken is not a  dinosaur but something that resembles  our timeline's nautilus  although our analog to this species  though interesting isn't nearly as  fantastical  and the last species we're touching on  is by far the largest  this is the walk a huge leviathan which  is a descendant of the ichthyosaur  that evolved to eat plankton this  massive species is of course  directly comparable to our world's  whales sharing similar feeding habits  and  gargantuan proportions what a creative  species  and that's the end of dougal dixon's the  new dinosaurs  like i said in the opening the new  dinosaurs might be highly speculative  but it's certainly interesting  if you ever get the chance to check out  more of his work i'd recommend it in the  meantime thanks for watching  if you like this entry into the archive  please lend your support  and like subscribe and hit the  notification icon to stay up to date on  all things curious  see you in the next video  you

